A capability is a reference you can call. [Why OCapN]($link.page('why'))
explains why that matters, and [modules]($link.page('modules')) shows where
`Session`, `Host`, and `Peer` fit.

# A capability is a reference

Code that holds a reference to an object can call it. Code that was never
handed one cannot, and there is no way to manufacture one from bytes.
[CapTP](https://web.archive.org/web/20250114141521/http://erights.org/elib/distrib/captp/) is the protocol that keeps
this true between two programs on a network.
[OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn) is its standardization.
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) is an implementation
of it, and the peer glowcap checks against.

Most permission features turn out to be ways of passing references around.
Hand one along and you have delegated. Wrap one in an object that forwards
only some methods, hand out the wrapper, and you have attenuated. Cut the
wrapper and you have revoked.
[Why OCapN]($link.page('why')) walks all three with pictures.

[A capability is a reference. Pass it as an argument and the partner can call it. Without it, the partner cannot reach the object at all.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-capability.svg'))

A call is a `deliver` message, and you immediately hold a promise for the
answer. When the partner has the answer, it sends a message back that settles
the promise. [How a promise settles]($link.page('protocol').ref('how-a-promise-settles'))
shows that message on the wire.

[A single call, start to finish: you deliver it and immediately hold a promise, and the partner's answer settles that promise to a result.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-call.svg'))

# Getting the first reference: locators and sturdyrefs

References only come from other references, so the first one needs a locator,
an address string:

```
ocapn://<designator>.<transport>
```

The **designator** names the peer: an iroh node id, a public key for Noise
and keyed transports, a Tor service id, or a `host:port` for testing-only
`tcp`. Optional `?key=value` **hints** carry routing help, like the socket
address for a keyed listener. Hints can only help you reach a peer. They
give no access of their own.

Dialing a locator opens a session. The **bootstrap object** is the one
capability available at first. You fetch a published name from it and get
back a live reference. From there, new references come from the ones you
already hold.

A **sturdyref** is a locator with a **swiss number** on the end, and it names
one object. It is a reference you can write down, for example in a config
file. Redeeming a sturdyref means dialing the peer and fetching that object.

The name you fetch is the swiss number. Anyone who can reach the peer and
knows the name can fetch the object, so a name is only as private as it is
hard to guess. The examples use short names like `"greeter"` for readability.
Give anything private a long random name.

A name can be published for one partner or for everyone. A `Session`
publication answers only the partner on that session. A Host-wide publication
answers every session, so an introduction can hand it to a third peer. When
both use the same name, the partner gets the one its own session published.

A sturdyref still works after a restart only if the peer comes back with the
same identity and can still answer for that swiss number. A plain `Host`
forgets its published names when the process exits, so the application
publishes them again when it starts.
[Loam]($link.page('loam')), glowcap's storage layer, keeps objects and their
names on disk, and restores an object when a peer fetches its name.

| Word | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| locator | A peer address, and how to dial it. |
| swiss number | The secret name of one object at that peer. |
| live reference | A reference you hold in a session and can call. |

[Everything left of the hints is an address, and none of it grants access. Only the swiss number names something you can be given.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-locator.svg'))

> **Swiss number**: holding the string *is* the permission. There is no
> permission list and no login. The name comes from the numbered Swiss bank
> account, where the number alone is the access.

The
[sturdyref diagrams](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/07-sturdyrefs-and-locators.md)
go deeper. [Transports]($link.page('transports')) explains what a locator can
name, and a [multi-home locator]($link.page('multihome')) carries several
transports under one key.

# Promise pipelining

A remote call hands back a **promise** before any bytes return, and you can
call methods on that promise right away. Those calls travel to the peer and
queue against the pending answer, so they run on the peer as soon as it
settles, with no extra round trip.

Ask a task board for a column, then ask that column for a card, and both
questions cross the network in one flight. Chapter 06,
[pipeline and attenuate](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/examples/zig/06_pipeline_and_attenuate.zig),
runs that, and the
[promises and pipelining](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/03-promises-and-pipelining.md)
diagrams draw it.

# Three-party handoff

Alice holds a reference to Carol's object and wants Bob to have it. Alice
could pass Bob's calls along to Carol herself, but then every call between
them would have to go through her. Instead, CapTP lets her write an
introduction that Bob presents to Carol himself. After that, Bob and Carol
talk directly.

The handoff runs in four stages:

1. Alice **deposits** a gift with Carol and builds a signed **give**.
2. Alice's answer to Bob carries that give (the introduction).
3. Bob **redeems** with Carol and gets a live reference.
4. Bob and Carol talk directly, without Alice.

In glowcap you never handle the gift bytes. When a call's answer is an
introduction, glowcap redeems it for you, and the call you were already
waiting on settles as the introduced capability. In the Rooms chat,
**Message *name* directly** is this kind of introduction: the room hands you a
live reference to the other member.

Chapter 02,
[introduce and redeem](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/examples/zig/02_introduce_and_redeem.zig),
runs the whole exchange, and the
[handoffs](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/06-handoffs.md)
diagrams draw it.

# Agreeing on a version and codec

Two peers have to agree on a CapTP **version** and a **codec** first. Together
those are the session's **dialect**. Each side
has a ranked menu, both apply the same rule to the two menus, and the most
preferred shared entry wins. If the menus share nothing, no session opens. The
hello itself always names the 1.0 baseline, and each side's menu rides along
as hints on the location it signs.

[Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup) and Cap'n Proto are codecs, two ways
of writing the same message down. Session logic never changes with the codec.
[Codecs]($link.page('codecs')) compares them.

[Both peers run the same rule over the two menus and reach the same pick.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-negotiation.svg'))

[The dialect depends on who answers: the standard baseline against Goblins, glowcap's own choices between two glowcap peers.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-dialects.svg'))

With a Goblins peer, the choice is CapTP 1.0 over Syrup, the shared baseline
for interop. If glowcap and Goblins disagree there, glowcap treats it as a
glowcap bug. Two glowcap peers can negotiate up to `glowcap-next`, an
experimental CapTP version of glowcap's own, usually with Cap'n Proto.
[What a CapTP 1.0 peer sees]($link.page('protocol')) lists the one place
application code can tell them apart.

[A friendly goblin sizing up the glowcap mark. glowcap tests its wire behavior against a real Goblins peer, so both sides have to understand the same conversation.]($image.siteAsset('glowcap-goblin-512.png'))

# Distributed GC

Each session keeps tables of the references it has exported and the answers
it is holding. An export slot counts how many times it was handed out. An
answer slot holds one reference and is released all at once.

When your program drops a far reference, distributed garbage collection tells
the peer with `op:gc-exports` or `op:gc-answers`, and the peer frees the unused
slot. Rust does this by default for every session on a Host, because `Drop`
runs at a known point. TypeScript, Python, and Racket release through
finalizers that run on their own schedule, so you turn it on per session. It
covers fetch results, fulfilled far answers, and capabilities nested inside
delivered arguments. When it is off, table entries stay until the session
ends. The
[GC](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/05-gc.md)
diagrams draw it.

The
[diagram catalog](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/protocol-diagrams/README.md)
shows the protocol flows in full. To write a client, go to
[Start building]($link.page('start')).
